    <section
    id="challenge"
    class="project-section w3-padding-64"
    aria-labelledby="challenge-heading">
        <h2 id="challenge-heading">The challenge</h2>

        <p class="w3-large">
            The teaching team needed a team-based consulting simulation that
            unfolded over multiple class days while still giving students a safe
            way to practice independently, and complete the simulation remotely with a team.
        </p>
        <p>
           The experience needed to release each level according to the course
            schedule, prevent teams from moving ahead early, and preserve the
            continuity of a consulting engagement across several sessions. At the
            same time, students needed a solo practice mode that would not interfere
            with their team’s official decisions or progress.
        </p>
       <p> The simulation also needed to respond meaningfully to accumulated
            decisions. It had to score strategic behavior without reducing the case
            to one correct answer, deliver different disruptions to different teams,
            generate personalized midpoint and final feedback, and remain usable
            when teams changed scribes or devices.</p>
             <p>
            Finally, the program office and teaching team needed a maintainable
            system. Future administrators had to be able to revise content,
            scoring, schedules, and feedback without reverse-engineering custom
            code or rebuilding the Qualtrics survey.
        </p>
            
    </section>

    <!-- Solution -->
    <section
    id="solution"
    class="project-section w3-padding-32"
    aria-labelledby="solution-heading">
        <h2 id="solution-heading">The solution</h2>

       <p>
            I designed and developed a multi-level Qualtrics simulation that places
            student teams in the role of consultants to a fictional organization.
        </p>

         <p>
            I created separate solo and team pathways. Solo mode allows students to
            explore the case independently and bypass selected collaborative
            requirements for practice. Team mode records the group’s official
            decisions, assigns a consistent curveball, and moves the team through
            the experience according to the course schedule.
        </p>

        <p>
            I also developed time-locking logic that controls when each level
            becomes available. This addressed a central instructional requirement:
            all teams could complete the experience over several days without
            accessing later developments before the teaching team intended to
            release them.
        </p>

        <p>
            Behind the interface, JavaScript manages cumulative scores, completed
            nodes, team assignments, recap logs, randomized content, midpoint
            diagnostics, final archetypes, and recovery behavior. Configuration
            data is maintained outside Qualtrics in CSV files, validated and
            transformed with R, and loaded as JSON at runtime.
        </p>

        <p>
            This architecture separates content from application logic. Faculty can
            revise question text, scores, curveballs, and archetype feedback without
            editing every Qualtrics question, while the core JavaScript remains
            reusable across the simulation.
        </p>


    </section>

