    <!-- Learning experience -->
    
<section
    id="learning-experience"
    class="project-section w3-padding-32"
    aria-labelledby="experience-heading">
        <h2 id="experience-heading">The learning experience</h2>
       <p>
            Students advise a fictional independent baseball organization seeking
            to become a higher-drawing regional attraction while improving a modest
            historical profit margin. Before entering the simulation, they review a
            case study and supporting fictional financial documents.
        </p>

        <p>
            The experience contains four levels with five decisions in each level.
            Each option contributes evidence across five strategic dimensions.
        </p>

        <p>
            The scoring model does not treat strategy as a single correct path.
            Choices represent different strengths and trade-offs, and teams’
            cumulative patterns determine the feedback they receive.
        </p>

        <p>
            A curveball introduces a regional development that forces each team to
            reconsider its direction. The team must decide whether the new
            information reinforces, challenges, or changes its strategy.
        </p>

        <p>
            Recap pages restore continuity across sessions by showing teams their
            earlier decisions. This is especially useful when scribe
            responsibilities rotate. A midpoint diagnostic describes the pattern
            emerging from the team’s choices, and the final debrief assigns one of
            five strategic archetypes.
        </p>

        <p>
            Each archetype includes a philosophy, core strengths, an inherent
            strategic trade-off, midpoint guidance, and an implementation-focused
            final debrief.
        </p>


    </section>





